@* A small floating glossary orb shown on the Saints browse and detail pages.
    It matches the shared floating-circle family (back-to-top, prayer guide, Summa guide, liturgical orb):
    a bottom-right circle that slides up to stack above the back-to-top arrow.
    Tapping it opens a Dialog explaining the saint facets &mdash; states of life, canonizations, eras,
    and patronages &mdash; so the terms used on the cards and detail pages are self-explanatory. *@

<FloatingGuide Title="Reading the saints" Eyebrow="What the labels mean" 
               AriaLabel="Glossary: what the saint labels mean" Tooltip="What the labels mean"
               DialogClass="dialog--glossary" Raised="Raised">
    <Icon>
        <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
             xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 6.5 22H20V2H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 4 4.5V19.5Z"
                  stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
        </svg>
    </Icon>
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="glossary">
            <p>
                Each saint is tagged with a few plain labels so you can browse and compare them. Here is
                what those labels mean.
            </p>

            <h3 class="guide-subhead">Era</h3>
            <p>
                The era places a saint in the sweep of the Church's history, from the apostolic age to
                our own day.
            </p>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><span class="guide-term">Early Church</span> &mdash; the apostolic age through the
                    Church Fathers and the great councils, roughly the first six centuries (up to about
                    600 AD).</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Medieval</span> &mdash; the Middle Ages, roughly 600 to
                    1500: the age of monasticism, the mendicant orders, and the scholastics.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Early modern</span> &mdash; roughly 1500 to 1800: the
                    Reformation, the Catholic Reformation, and the great missionary expansion.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Modern</span> &mdash; roughly 1800 to the present, from
                    the industrial age through the Second Vatican Council and our own day.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="guide-subhead">State of life</h3>
            <p>
                A saint's state of life is the vocation or office they lived. Holy orders build on one
                another &mdash; deacon, then priest, then bishop &mdash; so a saint often carries more
                than one. These labels are independent, and a saint may hold several.
            </p>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><span class="guide-term">Religious</span> &mdash; a vowed member of a religious
                    order or community (Franciscan, Carmelite, Benedictine, and so on), as distinct from
                    diocesan clergy.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Deacon</span> &mdash; the first rank of Holy Orders,
                    ordained to service of the word, the altar, and charity.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Priest</span> &mdash; ordained to offer the Mass,
                    forgive sins, and shepherd souls.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Bishop</span> &mdash; a successor of the Apostles who
                    holds the fullness of Holy Orders and governs a local church.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Pope</span> &mdash; the Bishop of Rome and successor of
                    St. Peter, who leads the whole Church. (An office, not a separate order.)</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Lay faithful</span> &mdash; a baptized member of the
                    Church who is not ordained, living holiness in the world.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Martyr</span> &mdash; one who was killed for the faith,
                    bearing the ultimate witness to Christ.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Virgin</span> &mdash; one who consecrated their virginity
                    to God for the sake of the Kingdom.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Widow</span> &mdash; one who, after the death of a
                    spouse, gave the rest of their life to God's service.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="guide-subhead">Canonization</h3>
            <p>
                Canonization is the Church's path to formally recognizing that a person is in heaven and
                may be honored publicly. These labels mark how far that recognition has advanced.
            </p>
            <ul class="guide-list">
                <li><span class="guide-term">Servant of God</span> &mdash; the first step, given once
                    the Church opens an official cause for the person.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Venerable</span> &mdash; declared to have lived heroic
                    virtue, though not yet beatified.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Beatified</span> &mdash; declared "Blessed," permitting
                    limited public devotion (usually after a verified miracle).</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Canonized</span> &mdash; declared a saint, honored by the
                    whole Church with the title "Saint."</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Doctor of the Church</span> &mdash; a saint recognized for
                    the outstanding value of their teaching for the whole Church.</li>
                <li><span class="guide-term">Pre-Congregation</span> &mdash; honored as a saint by
                    ancient tradition, from before the modern canonization process existed.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="guide-subhead">Patronage</h3>
            <p>
                A patron saint is a special heavenly intercessor and protector for a cause, place, trade,
                or group of people &mdash; often tied to something in the saint's own life. Patronages
                are a time-honored way of asking a particular saint to pray for a particular need; they
                are shown on each profile to help you find a saint close to your situation.
            </p>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Footer>
        <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="context.CloseAsync">Close</button>
    </Footer>
</FloatingGuide>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// When true, the orb sits one slot higher so it clears another floating control that already
    /// occupies the base slot (e.g. the liturgical orb on the Saints browse page).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Raised { get; set; }
}

